WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.299
For DC Comics fans, hopes have been growing
that Robin — Batman's legendary partner

00:00:05.299 --> 00:00:08.710
in crime fighting — might get his own solo
movie someday.

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:13.669
After all, Warner Bros. is hard at work adapting
tons of DC Comics characters for appearances

00:00:13.669 --> 00:00:15.570
in its shared cinematic universe.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:19.680
If they're gonna give Aquaman his own movie,
no character's really out of the question

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:20.680
at this point.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:25.250
And Robin's actually one of the most popular
and visible DC Comics characters ever!

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:29.700
It's tough to go very far without seeing our
favorite spandex sidekick on TV, whether it's

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:35.870
Teen Titans Go, Young Justice or in DC's animated
movies like Batman vs. Robin, and Justice

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:40.640
League vs. Teen Titans — or even as one
of the featured characters in 2017's Lego

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:41.880
Batman Movie.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:42.880
"Riiiip!

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:43.880
That's better!"

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:47.530
He's even been named the second best sidekick
ever — right behind Ed McMahon!

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:49.350
"What an exciting idea!"

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:50.989
(audience laughs)

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:52.890
But just don't get too excited.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:57.399
While Robin-related miracles can happen — Batman
did eventually let him wear pants — the

00:00:57.399 --> 00:01:01.320
truth is that DC has probably already killed
the chance of a Robin movie.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.480
And it didn't even require a crowbar!

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:04.530
He's already dead

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:09.659
Perhaps the most direct reason DC isn't going
to make a Robin movie is that, well...the

00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:11.180
character's already been killed.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:16.039
Robin's DC movie-verse death was first hinted
at in Batman v Superman, and then mentioned

00:01:16.039 --> 00:01:17.950
outright in Suicide Squad.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:22.399
Now, Batman goes through Robins like tissues,
so it's entirely possible that Warner Bros.

00:01:22.399 --> 00:01:26.380
could pick up from one of the nearly half-dozen
other characters who've been the Boy — or

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:27.380
Girl — Wonder.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.469
But that inflicts its own set of headaches...

00:01:29.469 --> 00:01:31.130
Which Robin to choose?

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:35.189
While Batman has pretty much always been Bruce
Wayne — with only a few brief exceptions

00:01:35.189 --> 00:01:38.779
— being Robin is basically the equivalent
of a superhero internship.

00:01:38.779 --> 00:01:45.040
There was Dick Grayson, Jason Todd, Tim Drake,
Stephanie Brown, and Damian Wayne, who's actually

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:46.859
Bruce Wayne's murder-happy son.

00:01:46.859 --> 00:01:50.420
Each choice would bring a certain amount of
baggage and convoluted backstory.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:55.289
And no matter the choice, it'll probably annoy
some segment of comic book readers whose favorite

00:01:55.289 --> 00:01:56.750
Robin wasn't picked.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:57.990
Too much backstory

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:02.349
But let's pretend that Warner Bros. decides
that movie Batman does need a movie Robin.

00:02:02.349 --> 00:02:06.950
The fact that Robin's already been killed
before the events of DC's new movies complicates

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:08.340
things even further.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:12.709
Is the dead Robin Jason Todd, mirroring his
comic book death at the Joker's hands?

00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:17.769
Was it Dick, the first Robin — or has he
already graduated to solo hero status as Nightwing?

00:02:17.769 --> 00:02:21.819
What's the best way to introduce a living
version of Robin into DC's movies without

00:02:21.819 --> 00:02:24.510
first delving into the twists and turns of
continuity?

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.810
Bringing in a new Robin would be complicated
enough in a Batman movie.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.739
Don't hold your breath on a solo outing.

00:02:29.739 --> 00:02:31.849
Boy, wonder how the fans really feel...

00:02:31.849 --> 00:02:36.200
Perhaps the most direct and blunt reason that
there won't be a Robin movie is that, well,

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:39.060
plenty of people may not actually want it
after all.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.060
And there's proof.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:44.269
When people talk about how Jason Todd was
killed by the Joker, that's only half true.

00:02:44.269 --> 00:02:50.780
In fact, the Joker had some accomplices — 5,343
comic book fans, to be exact.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:56.890
In 1988, DC Comics put Jason into mortal jeopardy
at the end of an issue, then included a 1-900

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:00.780
number — y'know, the kind that costs money
to call — to decide his fate.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:04.500
Even though only 72 more fans voted to kill
him off than those who called to keep him

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.930
alive, the fact that there wasn't an overwhelming
response for him to live kind of says something

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:12.239
about how much fans seemed to actually care
about Robin in general.

00:03:12.239 --> 00:03:13.560
And that's not all.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.340
Think about the fact that there have been
eight live action films starring Batman since

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:22.569
1989, and only two of them — Batman Forever
and Batman and Robin, widely known as the

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:26.230
crappiest two that fans hate the most — featured
the Boy Wonder.

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:30.799
Christopher Nolan's Batman trilogy performed
just fine without the added benefit of Robin.

00:03:30.799 --> 00:03:35.219
And, no, shoehorning a Robin reference into
the end of Dark Knight Rises doesn't count.

00:03:35.219 --> 00:03:39.459
"You should use your full name, I like that
name...Robin."

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:40.659
UGHHHHH.

00:03:41.069 --> 00:03:42.069
Teamwork

00:03:42.069 --> 00:03:46.579
Finally, and most importantly, the idea of
a Robin solo movie is pretty much impossible

00:03:46.579 --> 00:03:47.870
when you think about it.

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:51.939
Since it's pretty much baked into his character
from the start, Robin just works better as

00:03:51.939 --> 00:03:57.560
part of a team, whether it be in the Dynamic
Duo, or in a larger group like the Teen Titans.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:01.040
Sidekicks are usually just reflections of
the primary hero — so what would a Robin

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.849
movie be without Batman?

00:04:02.849 --> 00:04:07.329
Robin's first solo comic series in the '90s
shows Batman sending Tim Drake off to France

00:04:07.329 --> 00:04:11.099
to learn kung fu — because everyone knows
the best ninjas come from Paris.

00:04:11.099 --> 00:04:15.319
Robin's next solo series left him in Gotham
City to fight the Joker alone — while Batman

00:04:15.319 --> 00:04:16.840
took off to Rio de Janeiro.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:21.040
Would a Robin solo movie somehow contrive
to get Batman out of the picture in the same

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:21.540
way?

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:22.080
Just picture it.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:24.820
"Uh, hi Commissioner, it's me, Robin.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:28.600
Sorry, Batman's on vacation this week, so
he can't solve the horrible serial murderers

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:29.640
plaguing the city.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.080
Umm, but hey at least he'll get a good tan."

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:33.080
"What?!

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.060
Surely you must be jesting!"

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:37.750
"No, I'm not jesting, sorry.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:40.720
But, like, I'll totally do the voice if you
think that'll help.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.500
*Ahem*: I am the night!

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:44.360
I am Batmaaaan!"

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:50.100
"Ohhh...catastrophic!"

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:54.320
At this rate, it'd be surprising if Robin
ever shows up in the DC Extended Universe

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:57.650
at all, let alone in a starring role in his
own superhero flick.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:01.510
Then again, someone thought it was a good
idea to make a movie about Superman knock-off

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:04.200
Steel, starring a rapping basketball player:

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:05.320
"It's hammer time!"

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:07.530
"Aahh!!"

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:09.610
So, really, anything's possible.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:10.610
Thanks for watching!

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:13.520
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube Channel.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:15.590
Plus, check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love too!

